\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 The app removes rejected input values from validation error responses so buyer details are not echoed unnecessarily. Request logs use route templates instead of order IDs and omit bodies. Uvicorn access logging is disabled in the configured command.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Authentication uses HTTP Basic for one merchant. It needs HTTPS when used remotely. CORS lets the configured browser origin send permitted requests; it does not authenticate a merchant and does not provide tenant isolation.\par
\pard\s2\f0\fs30\b\sb260\sa130\keepn 12 A complete local API walkthrough\b0\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Use synthetic records for this exercise. Run the setup and seed steps first. Seeding inserts DEMO-CLEAN, DEMO-MISMATCH and DEMO-UNCERTAIN and does not overwrite existing orders.\par
\pard\s3\f0\fs24\b\sb180\sa100\keepn Observe the three seeded outcomes\b0\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 In /docs, authorize and call GET /orders. You should find one PROTECTED, one BLOCKED and one REVIEW order when the seed database is fresh. The synthetic tracking and source fields must stay labeled as simulated or fixture data.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Open GET /orders/DEMO-MISMATCH. Compare paypal_address.line1 with label.address.line1. The house number changes from 123 to 987. The comparison should report MISMATCH and a reason describing a different number.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Open GET /orders/DEMO-UNCERTAIN. Its label confidence is 0.65. Even if the address agrees, the verdict should be REVIEW because confidence is below 0.90.\par
\pard\s3\f0\fs24\b\sb180\sa100\keepn Create a new mismatch through the label route\b0\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Call POST /labels with the parsed-label example from section 8 for DEMO-CLEAN. It changes that fixture order's latest label to 987 Main Street. The response should show MISMATCH, BLOCKED and HOLD_REFUND_REORDER if the other current inputs remain eligible and confident.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Then post the original 123 Main Street address with confidence 0.98. The result should return to PROTECTED, and the database should retain both verdict snapshots. There is currently no public history endpoint, so inspect those records through your tests or database tools.\par
\pard\s3\f0\fs24\b\sb180\sa100\keepn Exercise human review\b0\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Post another label with the original address and confidence 0.65. The verdict should be REVIEW. Repeat with confidence omitted and expect REVIEW again. Do not convert missing confidence to an artificial 1.0 in an adapter.\par
\pard\s3\f0\fs24\b\sb180\sa100\keepn Exercise validation and conflicts\b0\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Try confidence 9 and expect 422. Try an unknown order ID and expect 404. Send a quote that is not present in the message and expect 422. Reuse a stored message ID with different text while retaining a valid matching quote and expect 409.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 These calls change the fixture orders. The seed command will not reset them because it preserves existing orders. For a pristine recording, use a new disposable demo database or a separately designed, reviewed reset procedure. Do not delete shared records casually.\par
\pard\s2\f0\fs30\b\sb260\sa130\keepn 13 Your daily development workflow\b0\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Begin with the demo path and current contract. Pick one observable outcome, such as making a particular valid formatting variant produce NEAR_MATCH. Read the shared function and its callers before editing it.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Add a focused test when the behavior is nontrivial. Make the change in the owning layer, run the relevant tests, then run the backend suite. If the contract changes, update schemas, fixtures, examples and affected frontend or integration callers together.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Re-run one meaningful API scenario after changes to orchestration, serialization or persistence. A passing pure rule test does not prove the response model or transaction path works. A passing SQLite API test does not prove the Postgres concurrency path works.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Keep source code changes separate from deployment and data operations. Review migrations before applying them to a shared database. Never remove an original transaction address to accommodate a customer's new request.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 At the end of the day, show the team a working scenario, report remaining integration boundaries and provide exact payload examples. A short demonstration of an explained verdict is more useful than reporting that a module is almost done.\par
\pard\s3\f0\fs24\b\sb180\sa100\keepn Build order if you are starting from scratch\b0\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 1. Agree US scope, JSON contracts, null handling and rule precedence.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 2. Create the FastAPI app, database models and migration.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 3. Ingest one synthetic order through the shared helper.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 4. Accept parsed label JSON and implement the plain comparison function.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 5. Persist the verdict and return the order through the API.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 6. Cover formatting variants, destination changes and uncertainty with tests.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 7. Connect Member 1 and Member 2 to the same contracts.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 8. Connect Member 4 to queue, detail and mutation responses.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 9. Verify Postgres behavior, deploy and rehearse the actual demo.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 10. Add approved actions and evidence only after the core path is stable.\par
\pard\s2\f0\fs30\b\sb260\sa130\keepn 14 Testing without destroying the wrong database\b0\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 The suite contains direct rule tests and API tests. The API fixture runs against SQLite and optionally Postgres. Read tests/conftest.py before setting TEST_DATABASE_URL.\par
\pard\s0\f1\fs18\li120\sa0\sl220\slmult0\keep python -m pytest -q\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 Without TEST_DATABASE_URL, Postgres variants skip. A green run with skipped Postgres tests is not evidence that hosted Postgres behavior or row locking was checked.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 The Postgres test fixture runs migrations and deletes all rows in the four application tables after each test. TEST_DATABASE_URL must point to a separate, disposable test database. Never use the local working demo, a teammate's shared database or the deployed database for this variable.\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 With the local Compose database running, create a separate database, then opt in:\par
\pard\s0\f1\fs18\li120\sa0\sl220\slmult0\keep\keepn docker compose exec postgres createdb -U sellershield sellershield_test\par
\pard\s0\f1\fs18\li120\sa0\sl220\slmult0\keep\keepn $env:TEST_DATABASE_URL = 'postgresql+psycopg://sellershield:local-only@localhost:5432/sellershield_test'\par
\pard\s0\f1\fs18\li120\sa0\sl220\slmult0\keep python -m pytest -q\par
\pard\s0\f0\fs22\keep\sa120\sl270\slmult0 If the test database already exists, reuse it only when it is disposable and not shared. Remove the test variable from a shell after testing when appropriate:\par
\pard\s0\f1\fs18\li120\sa0\sl220\slmult0\keep Remove-Item Env:TEST_DATABASE_URL\par
